Begin geology with sustained reading and a gradual practical progression (@geo-reading: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @geology, #source "../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX", #source "../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD", #source "../sources/Geodiversidade-do-Brasil.lingua#r_2ZQN8TT3KZYS9MCFGZV5A1V682", #source "../sources/Introductory-Geology-Laboratory-Manual.lingua#r_7G68RZ432EQWTB84DSVJ4JCZ2Z", #geology "A-geological-question.lingua#r_4E4VEKFKRQKTEWG4VN53KM9SHA") { r_3H533XF25V4VZX2GZ149Q8M6KQ
**Read:** [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), chapters 1–2 in full first, then the complete book; [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), the complete book alongside the history sequence; [Geodiversidade do Brasil — Cassio Roberto da Silva and contributors](../sources/Geodiversidade-do-Brasil.lingua#r_2ZQN8TT3KZYS9MCFGZV5A1V682), the complete book in parallel; [Laboratory Manual for Introductory Geology — Deline, Harris, and Tefend](../sources/Introductory-Geology-Laboratory-Manual.lingua#r_7G68RZ432EQWTB84DSVJ4JCZ2Z), the complete relevant labs as the sequence develops.

Start with Physical Geology chapters 1–2, reading complete explanations, figures, examples, and exercises. Read the Brazilian book's introduction in parallel if Portuguese helps. Learn the terms through actual observations and drawings rather than trying to memorize the entire glossary before seeing a rock or map.

Continue through all 22 chapters in the sequence developed here, revisiting the matching book sections when this trail brings time, tectonics, structures, landscapes, and water together differently. Read the whole historical-geology book alongside the chronological sequence. Complete chapters and whole-book reading provide the substance; these Records explain how the parts connect and which observations or calculations deserve closer work.

Use the introductory laboratory manual throughout. Complete the structural book and ten labs once map/orientation foundations are clear. The full MIT course offers another lecture/exercise progression. Portuguese Geodiversidade do Brasil and the complete Rio Grande do Sul volume provide sustained regional contexts, with different purposes and dates preserved.

Follow shared quantitative, chemical, measurement, or computational Records at the step using them. Their own continuations remain available, and their geology connections return to the relevant question. The advanced geochronology notes, full papers, mapping methods, and independent studies demand greater mathematical, practical, and scholarly work later.

**Work:** begin a described observation and question now. Keep original sketches, calculations, explanations, and revisions in your study workspace. Later completion requires substantial book/course/lab work and independent evidence-based studies, while entering or following a Record remains simple.

**Continue:** [Begin with a rock, a landscape, and a question about its history](A-geological-question.lingua#r_4E4VEKFKRQKTEWG4VN53KM9SHA).

